\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Enlarging the right subgroup of a double coset}

\p{Let #{G} be a group and let #{\Gamma,K,K'} be subgroups of
#{G}, with #{K\subseteq K'}. Write #{[x]_K} for the
\vocabk{double coset}{fcap-004P} #{\Gamma xK}; throughout,
#{\Gamma} is fixed. There is a surjection

##{\begin{aligned}
p_{K,K'}:\Gamma\backslash G/K&\longrightarrow\Gamma\backslash G/K',\\
[x]_K&\longmapsto[x]_{K'}.
\end{aligned}}

Thus allowing more right transformations can identify classes that
were previously distinct.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{If #{[x]_K=[y]_K}, there are #{\gamma\in\Gamma} and
#{k\in K} with #{y=\gamma xk}. The inclusion puts the same
#{k} in #{K'}, so the same witnesses give #{[x]_{K'}=[y]_{K'}}.
The assignment therefore does not depend on the representative.
For any target class #{c}, choose a representative #{x\in G}
with #{c=[x]_{K'}}. Then #{[x]_K} is a source class and
#{p_{K,K'}([x]_K)=c}, proving surjectivity.}
}

\p{Injectivity fails even for the group #{C_2=\{1,s\}}, where
#{s^2=1} and #{s\ne1}. Take #{\Gamma=K=\{1\}} and #{K'=C_2}.
The source classes are exactly #{\{1\}} and #{\{s\}}: with
both witnesses equal to #{1}, two representatives are equivalent
only when they are equal. The target has the single class #{C_2},
because #{\Gamma xK'=xC_2=C_2} for each #{x}. Hence precisely
two source classes map to one target class. This is a general-group
counterexample, without an asserted adelic realization.}

\p{For the \vocabk{finite adelic class sets}{fcap-004P}, fix a
nondegenerate quadratic form #{Q} on a finite-dimensional rational space #{V}
and one \vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C}
#{U}. Apply the proposition separately with

##{(G,\Gamma,K)=
\bigl(H_f(U),\delta_f^H(H(V,Q)),K_H\bigr),
\qquad H\in\{O,SO,\Spin\}.}

Here the ambient restricted group is from \ref{fcap-004F},
the rational image is from \ref{fcap-004K}, and #{K_H} is the
actual product reference subgroup. Any subgroup #{K'_H} of that
same #{H_f(U)} containing #{K_H} gives the stated surjection.
An enlargement of reference families does not by itself keep the
ambient restricted product fixed; the proposition applies only
after the ambient group and the rational diagonal have been identified.}

\p{The adelic objects have classical context in
\citet{Example 2.3, p. 3, and Theorem 3.6, pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}.
The quotient map and counterexample above are elementary group
arguments, not assertions quoted from that source. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{DoubleCoset.quotientMapOfLERight_surjective}
formalizes the general surjectivity statement. Its separate adelic
specializations are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicOrthogonalDoubleCosetMapOfLE_surjective},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDoubleCosetMapOfLE_surjective}
and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpinDoubleCosetMapOfLE_surjective}.
No finiteness of these quotient sets is needed.}
